        "id": "ch13_7_1-2",
        "text": "I The second line sets the workgroup name, in our case the Windows default name, WORKGROUP\nI The third line determines that it will not be necessary to have a Samba user account in order\nto access the shares (mapping the guest to Bad User )\nI Guest users are allowed on the fourth line\nI Then we set the interfaces used by Samba, which in our case will be the Ethernet connection\n( eno1 ) and the loopback ( lo ) interfaces (check your exact interface name with the ip\naddr show or ip link commands)\nI We will set the server role and the hosts allowed (from the local pool)\nI The last line sets the order the hostnames are checked, using the broadcast ( bcast ) method\n6.\nNow we will create the [shares] directive and add the necessary configuration options\nfor the shares. We will add details about the shared directory on our server, user details, and\npermissions. See the following screenshot for details:\nFigure 13.21 \u2013 Adding information inside the shares.conf file\nLet us briefly explain the contents of this directive:\nI We first set a name for the shares\nI Then we set the path of our local shared directory\nI Next, we set the default permissions for the directory and files\nI We also set the default mask values\nI Finally, we set the shares as public, guest-user friendly, and writable",
